# Commits to skip when running `git blame`.
#
# Only tree-wide formatter runs belong here: commits where every changed file
# is byte-identical to its parent once whitespace, trailing commas and key
# order are normalised away. A commit that also changes behaviour must not be
# listed, because this file is per-commit and cannot ignore a commit for one
# file while honouring it for another — listing a mixed commit hides its real
# change from blame entirely.
#
# `.rustfmt.toml` and `.taplo.toml` both align on content width
# (struct_field_align_threshold, align_entries), and `.taplo.toml` reorders
# keys. That means a one-line edit to a long entry reformats every sibling
# around it, so these commits will keep appearing.
#
# GitHub reads this file automatically in its blame view:
#   https://docs.github.com/en/repositories/working-with-files/using-files/viewing-a-file#ignore-commits-in-the-blame-view
#
# To use it locally:
#   git config --local blame.ignoreRevsFile .git-blame-ignore-revs
# To stop using it:
#   git config --local --unset blame.ignoreRevsFile
#
# Before adding an entry, confirm the commit is formatting-only:
#   git show -w --format='' <sha> | grep -E '^[+-][^+-]'
# and check it reorder-insensitively, since taplo sorts keys:
#   for f in $(git show --name-only --format='' <sha>); do
#     diff <(git show <sha>^:$f | tr -d '[:blank:]' | tr -d ',' | sort) \
#          <(git show <sha>:$f  | tr -d '[:blank:]' | tr -d ',' | sort)
#   done
#
# No entries yet — this template has no commit history of its own. Add
# formatting-only commit SHAs here as they land.
